<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torghut</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Torgut</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Torghut are an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> Mongol tribe. In the early 17th century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kho-orluk">Kho Orluk</a>, many of them migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> across the Kazakh steppe to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, where they founded the Kalmyk Khanate. In 1771 most of the Volga Torghut, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubashi-khan">Ubashi Khan</a>, made a disastrous trek back to Dzungaria under the Qing. Torghut communities live today in Kalmykia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torghut.jpg" alt="Torghut" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khiruge · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Oirat_Confederation.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
